\section{Make Values Once, Not Storage Repeatedly}

Single assignment means that one dynamic name denotes one value. It does not mean that a loop cannot produce many values: each iteration can create a distinct instance, often represented by an SSA version, an iteration tag, or an array element. This removes write--write races on those values and exposes dependencies, but it does not by itself guarantee determinism. Nondeterministic input choice, unordered merge, exceptions, external effects, or racing stateful actors can still distinguish executions.

Pure dataflow nodes create new tokens rather than modifying old ones, which supports determinate results when channels are ordered and operators are deterministic \cite{johnston2004dataflow}. State must not be hidden to recover this property. A delay, recurrence edge, state token, or explicitly encapsulated actor state says which old value a firing observes.

\section{Express a Recurrence Without Mutation}

This SISAL-like fragment binds each result once and constructs a new array:

\begin{verbatim}
function Scale(A : array[integer]; k : integer
               returns array[integer])
  for a in A
    returns array of a * k
  end for
end function
\end{verbatim}

The source does not update \texttt{A}; each iteration contributes one element to the result. An accumulator can similarly be represented by a loop-carried value whose instance at iteration \(i+1\) depends on instance \(i\). \Cref{fig:visual-354} contrasts explicit recurrence with accidental shared storage.

