% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Name Dynamic Instances in the IR}

\begin{verbatim}
for each source binding b:
    create value node V(b, lexical_instance)
for each loop-carried binding s:
    create init node S0
    create recurrence edge S(i) -> S(i+1)
    attach iteration tag i to dynamic tokens
reject a second producer for the same value instance
\end{verbatim}

The distinction between a static source name and a dynamic value instance is essential. Reusing one graph edge for every loop iteration without tags or a disciplined feedback protocol allows operands from different iterations to meet.

\section{Carry State and Effects Explicitly}

The IR should contain operation and region nodes, value and control/recurrence edges, explicit state or memory tokens, loop and branch joins, clocks or token rates where applicable, value types, effect rows, and source provenance. Compilation can produce SSA-based machine IR, parallel task graphs, vector loops, or a dataflow runtime graph. Array uniqueness information may permit in-place storage reuse even though the language-level array remains immutable.

Legal optimizations include copy propagation, common-subexpression elimination, parallel evaluation, and storage reuse, but only when types, arithmetic behavior, exceptions, and effect ordering agree. Semantic-preservation risks include aliasing an old array after ``reusing'' it, merging two effectful calls, or reassociating floating-point reductions. Preservation concerns the single-assignment value graph and explicit effect order, not retaining a fresh physical allocation for every source value.

\section{Decide Whether a Physical Cell Can Be Reused}

Consider a value array initialized to \([4,7]\). Construct \(B\) from \(A\) by replacing its first element with \(9\), then return both \(A[0]\) and \(B[0]\). The result is \((4,9)\); it is not \((9,9)\). The replacement creates a new abstract array identity even if the runtime sometimes chooses to mutate a backing buffer. This matters for aliases as well: if \(C\) is a view of \(A\) captured before the replacement, a later read of \(C[0]\) is still a use of the old storage.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Later observation & Reuse candidate? & Reason \\
\hline
Only \(B[0]\) & Yes & Old element no longer live \\
\(A[0]\) and \(B[0]\) & No & Both versions needed \\
Old slice through a closure & Not by name alone & Alias may escape \\
Old array in pending worker & Not yet & Worker can still read it \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
lower_replace(old, index, element, point):
    require check_bounds(index, old.shape)
    readers := possible_future_readers(old.storage, point)
    if readers is empty and exclusive_owner(old.storage):
        result_storage := old.storage
    else:
        result_storage := copy(old.storage)
    write(result_storage, index, element)
    return bind_fresh_array_value(result_storage, old.shape)
\end{verbatim}

This is a conservative whole-buffer rule; a more precise analysis can prove that no reader sees the modified part even if some reader still uses a disjoint slice. As a test, return an array and a closure capturing the original first element, then call the closure after the update. If the implementation chooses to save that one scalar separately before writing, it can optimize the storage without changing the source observation. When parallel iterations each construct a distinct result slot, the relevant proof instead relates every physical write to exactly one logical index and checks that initialization and ownership precede publishing the array.

\paragraph{Track the lifetime of a pending read.}
Suppose a task receives an immutable slice and is then suspended. A compiler
that considers the slice dead at the call site can still be wrong: the task
may resume after the parent has overwritten the array. Model the handoff
as a loan that ends when the task joins or explicitly gives up its view.
If the task can escape, do not infer that the loan ends at the lexical
scope of the spawning expression. In a deterministic test, pause a reader
after handing it the slice, perform the replacement, then resume it and
check that it returns the original value. This exercises storage
liveness separately from the single-assignment property of the graph.
